/* Linker version script: export the libretro API and nothing else.
 *
 * WHY THIS EXISTS
 * The core statically links ffmpeg, and without this it exported ~14,900
 * dynamic symbols, 609 of them ffmpeg's (avcodec_ and av_ prefixes). Note the
 * prefixes are written without a trailing wildcard on purpose: a "star slash"
 * inside this comment would close it early and corrupt the script, which is
 * exactly what happened on the first attempt. A libretro core is dlopen'd
 * into a frontend that often has its own ffmpeg (RetroArch does). When two
 * definitions of avcodec_alloc_context3 are in the global scope, the dynamic
 * linker binds our *calls* to the host's *implementation* - different version,
 * different struct layouts - and the first real decode walks off into memory
 * that is not what it thinks it is.
 *
 * That is not theoretical. It segfaulted on 2026-08-02, the first time the
 * Linux core was ever loaded into a frontend:
 *
 *   #0  libavcodec.so.61.19.101              <- the frontend's ffmpeg
 *   #2  xe::apu::XmaContextOld::PrepareDecoder   <- our call
 *   #5  xe::apu::XmaDecoder::WorkerThreadMain
 *
 * Making those symbols local means our calls can no longer be preempted, so
 * they bind to our own implementations. It also stops the core from polluting
 * the frontend's symbol namespace, which is good manners regardless.
 *
 * ELF only. Windows DLLs bind imports at link time and were never affected -
 * which is why months of Windows testing never saw this.
 */
{
  global:
    retro_*;
  local:
    *;
};
